in today's warning rounds a recent study  found some common drugs could increase  the risk of dementia or dementia-like  symptoms the study in JAMA International  Medicine discovered a nearly 50%  increase in odds of dementia in people  55 and older who took anti colon jarric  medications that type of drug is used to  treat a wide range of conditions  including depression symptoms of  Parkinson's disease of bladder control  and insomnia we reached out to the  makers of antidepressant Paxil for  comment but have not heard back an  estimated one in four older adults take  anti colon jerok drugs some  antihistamines like benadryl are also  anti colon jarek's but were not  associated with dementia in this study  CBS News medical contributor dr. Tara  Narula joins us now so Tara this can be  confusing for people because this word  anti colon Jarek is the first time  they've ever heard of it's very hard to  understand what that is but this is a  very broad class of medications and  really what we're talking about are  drugs that block a neurotransmitter or a  chemical messenger called  acetylcholine right this is one of the  first neurotransmitter discovered very  widespread in the body throughout the  central and peripheral nervous system  and it has a lot of functions it dilates  blood vessels it regulates muscle  contractions bodily secretions heart  rate and in the brain it has effects on  attention concentration and memory and  like you said a lot of Americans use  these drugs whether prescription or  over-the-counter ranging from things  like antihistamines or anti allergy  medicines sleep aids bladder control  medications Parkinson's drugs COPD meds  I mean the list goes on and on so how  significantly can anticholinergics  increase the risk of dementia well this  is very interesting so we knew about  some of the short-term risks associated  so these drugs can cause things in the  short-term like confusion delirium  memory changes but the idea was that if  you come off of them it may be  reversible in the mid-2000s more and  more research started coming out saying  well actually there might be long term  effects where it may be increasing your  risk of dementia and as you mentioned  there was a study just last month that  looked over to at over 200,000  individuals in Britain who took a strong  anti  cholinergic drug for three years and in  fact found a 49% increased risk of  dementia nine percent forty nine but  it's important to point out as with a  lot of studies this was not a cause and  effect studies right so most of these  studies have just shown an association  it's an observational type study in  addition a lot of researchers have said  look some of these drugs that were  giving the antidepressants and the sleep  aids we're giving to people who may have  had dementia all along because some of  these things like depression and sleep  loss are signs early signs of dementia  so it may not be the drug themselves  that's causing this so there's a lot of  these combinations of drugs seem to be  the root cause of the problem my  question is like with so many doctors  making so many prescriptions really it  seems like the buck stops with your  pharmacist  well the pharmacist and the doctor and  the patient when everybody needs to be  informed about this and the fact is that  elderly are more susceptible to these  types of problems and there are a lot of  reasons why we say that you know we know  that the blood-brain barrier that  permeability or the ability for  medications to cross increases as we get  older the amount of acetylcholine that  neurotransmitter decreases in our brain  as we get older so you can imagine if  you're now giving a drug that blocks it  you're gonna have a stronger effect the  body may not process the acetylcholine  as well and a lot of the elderly may be  on multiple medications so it's a  cumulative effect we know if you if you  stop taking these drugs that the effects  reverse themselves well this is just a  growing body of research right as I said  this is Association so what researchers  have said is what we really need is a  randomized control trial which is the  gold standard type where we take people  who are on these medications and then D  prescribed we take them off and we see  what happens to their risk of dementia  but really important is just to speak to  your doctor right and every visit you  should be going over all your  medications and saying do I need to be  on this is it really working for me and  what are the potential risks and  benefits are there alternative agents  that might be good for me and don't  assume if it's over-the-counter that  it's safe it may not be right benadryl  some of these other medications you can  buy over-the-counter Tylenol PM Advil PM  if you're a patient talk to your doctor  but if you're a care taker you might  want to review the medicine that the  person in your charge is taking one of  the cases described in the literature  was really remarkable woman went from  severe dementia changed her medications  suddenly she's back to a  functioning right and so patients with  dementia should really not be on these  medications at all so yes important if  you're a caretaker as well  all right Tara thank you very much
 

